    <DataNode elementId="Levan" textLabel="a levan" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="16703" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>Levan is a non-structural polysaccharide present in several microorganisms and a few plant species. It consists almost solely of fructosyl residues linked via &amp;beta;-2,6 linkages, and is packed into nano-sized spherical forms, giving it a very low intrinsic viscosity. Levan is a main ingredient in natto, a traditional Japanese food. Plants synthesize levan in the vacuole by the concerted action of various fructosyltransferases such as |FRAME: EC-2.4.1.10|. When acting alone, this enzyme synthesizes linear levan chains from sucrose via the trisaccharide intermediate |FRAME: 6-KESTOSE| (it can also use |FRAME: 1-KESTOTRIOSE| and |FRAME: NEOKESTOSE| as the initial fructose acceptors). Plant fructans generally have a low degree of polymerization (DP &lt;10 to 100). The occurrence of levansucrase is widespread in Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. Bacterial levans reach much higher molecular masses and contain up to 10,000 fructosyl units. Species that belong to the |FRAME: TAX-1301|, |FRAME: TAX-1243|, and |FRAME: TAX-1578| genera also contain |FRAME: EC-2.4.1.9|, which forms &amp;beta;-(2,1) fructosyl-fructose linkages. When expressed together with levansucrase, that enzyme creates branching points that connect multiple &amp;beta;-(2,6) linked levan chains, forming a branched polymer known as |FRAME: Graminans graminan| |CITS: [27178733]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="Levan" />
